---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../config';
import levels from '../../data/sight-words.json';

const title = `Dolch Sight Words Lists + Free Flash Cards | ${SITE.name}`;
const description = 'Free Dolch sight words lists for pre-K to 3rd grade, each with a printable sight word worksheet and flash cards. Pick words, print, practice.';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How many sight words should a kindergartner know?', a: 'There is no single number. Schools set their own goals, and they vary a lot from one district to the next, so the most reliable answer comes from your child’s teacher. Many kindergarten classes work through some or all of the Dolch pre-primer and primer lists during the year.' },
  { q: 'What’s the difference between Dolch and Fry sight words?', a: 'Dolch is a shorter list of 220 high-frequency words plus 95 nouns, grouped by grade from pre-K to 3rd grade. Fry is a longer list of 1,000 common words ranked by how often they appear, usually taught in groups of 100. The two lists overlap a lot, especially at the start.' },
  { q: 'Are Dolch words still used?', a: 'Yes. The lists are decades old, but the words themselves have not changed much, and many classrooms and reading programs still use Dolch lists today. Some schools use Fry or their own lists instead, so check with your child’s teacher if you want to match class work.' },
];
const url = new URL('/sight-words/', SITE.url).href;
const total = levels.reduce((n, l) => n + l.words.length, 0);
const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'CollectionPage',
    name: 'Sight words lists by grade',
    url,
    description,
    hasPart: levels.map((l) => ({ '@type': 'LearningResource', name: l.title, url: `${SITE.url}/sight-words/${l.slug}/` })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url + '/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Sight Words', item: url },
    ],
  },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) },
];
const shown = (w: string) => (w === 'I' ? w : w.toLowerCase());
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="pt-6 text-[14px] text-muted">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-ink">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span aria-current="page">Sight words</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="pt-6 pb-8 max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.8rem] font-bold"><span class="ruled">Dolch sight words lists by grade, with free worksheets and flash cards</span></h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted">Pick a level for the full word list, a sight word worksheet and printable flash cards.</p>
    </header>

    <ul class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {levels.map((l) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/sight-words/${l.slug}/`} class="block h-full rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-white p-5 hover:border-rule">
            <span class="block text-xl font-bold text-ink">{l.short} sight words</span>
            <span class="block mt-1 text-[14px] text-muted">Dolch {l.dolch.toLowerCase()} list, {l.words.length} words</span>
            <span class="block mt-3 text-lg text-ink">{l.words.slice(0, 6).map(shown).join(', ')}…</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <article class="prose-tl max-w-[68ch] mt-12">
      <p class="text-[1.15rem]!">
        Sight words are the short, common words kids meet on almost every page, like the, said and was. Many don't follow the usual
        phonics rules, so children learn to read them at a glance. These pages use the Dolch lists, a public-domain set of {total} words
        sorted into five levels from pre-K to 3rd grade.
      </p>

      <h2>What are the Dolch sight words?</h2>
      <p>
        Edward William Dolch compiled the lists in the 1930s and 1940s from words that come up again and again in children's books. They are
        still common in classrooms today. Some schools use a different list, such as Fry, or their own order. If you want to match class work,
        ask your child's teacher which words they are on.
      </p>

      <h2>Dolch vs Fry sight words</h2>
      <p>
        Both lists are made of the words kids meet most often in print, and they overlap a lot. The <strong>Dolch list</strong> was compiled
        by Edward William Dolch and published in 1936, then in his 1948 book <em>Problems in Reading</em>. It has 220 "service words"
        (the, said, because) plus a separate list of 95 nouns, and it's grouped by level from pre-primer to 3rd grade. The <strong>Fry list</strong> was put together by Edward Fry in the 1950s and updated in 1980. It ranks the 1,000 most common words in
        English reading material and is usually taught 100 words at a time. Dolch is shorter and sorted by grade, which makes it a handy
        starting point at home. Fry goes further, into later grades.
      </p>

      <h2>How to practice sight words at home</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Start small.</strong> Pick 3 to 5 words, not the whole list.</li>
        <li><strong>Trace, then write.</strong> Each level has a word tracing worksheet with one word per row and blank rows to finish.</li>
        <li><strong>Flash cards for speed.</strong> Print the cards, cut on the dashed lines, and read a few each day.</li>
        <li><strong>Find them in books.</strong> Spotting a word in a real story is what makes it stick.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep it short.</strong> Five minutes a day beats a long session once a week.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Kids learn to read at different speeds. If you have concerns, ask your child's teacher or pediatrician.</p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="divide-y divide-hairline border-y border-hairline">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="group py-4">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between gap-4 font-bold text-[1.075rem] text-ink">{f.q}<span aria-hidden="true" class="text-muted group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span></summary>
            <p class="mt-3 mb-0!">{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <h2>Still working on letters?</h2>
      <p>
        If letters are still new, start with our <a href="/letter-tracing/">letter tracing worksheets</a>, or make a <a href="/name-tracing/">name tracing worksheet</a> so the first word your child writes is their own.
      </p>
    </article>

    <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="full-list">
      <h2 id="full-list" class="text-[1.6rem] font-bold">Printable Dolch sight words list</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted max-w-[68ch]">All {total} Dolch sight words, grouped by level. Print this page for a quick reference, or open a level for its worksheet and flash cards.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {levels.map((l) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-bold"><a href={`/sight-words/${l.slug}/`} class="text-ink underline decoration-rule decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-mid">{l.short}</a> <span class="text-[14px] font-normal text-muted">Dolch {l.dolch.toLowerCase()}, {l.words.length} words</span></h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed text-ink">{l.words.map(shown).join(', ')}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
